---
// Figure: two ways a failover loses a delete the primary acknowledged.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import './repl.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="rp-lost-delete" title="An acknowledged delete, lost at failover" controls="steps"
  caption="A sequence of steps, not a timed simulation. I reproduced the crash on Redis 8.10.3 with a primary and a replica on one laptop: the replica was frozen with SIGSTOP while the primary acknowledged 9 MB of writes and then the delete, the primary was killed, and the replica was resumed and promoted. Every reply shown in the crash version was observed. The partition version follows the example in the Redis Sentinel documentation; I did not run it.">
  <Choice slot="params" name="cause" label="The primary" value="crash" options={[{ value: 'crash', label: 'crashes' }, { value: 'partition', label: 'is cut off by the network' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>A primary and its replica both hold the kettle at £39. The replica falls behind. The application deletes the kettle on the primary, which replies 1 straight away. The primary then crashes before it has sent the delete down the replication stream. The replica is promoted, and reads of the kettle return £39 with almost an hour left to live: the delete was acknowledged and then lost. WAIT 1 500, sent after the delete, would have returned 0, telling the application that no replica had the delete yet.</p>
    <p>In the second version a network fault cuts the primary, and one application server, off from the Sentinels and the replica. The Sentinels promote the replica, but the cut-off application server keeps using the old primary, which deletes the kettle and replies 1. When the fault heals, Sentinel turns the old primary into a replica of the new one, and it discards its own data, the delete included. Everyone then reads the kettle at £39.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { switchSequence } from './switch-sequence.mjs';
  import { failoverSpec, partitionSpec } from './sequences.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="rp-lost-delete"]').forEach(root => switchSequence(root, { crash: failoverSpec, partition: partitionSpec }, 'cause'));
</script>
